>  If so, is DCE broken into separate packages if
>a company only wants to purchase/implement the security part of DCE?

You cannot use DCE security without having the DCE RPC runtime.  It also
becomes awkward to use DCE security without having the DCE directory
services, since security really wants to put things into the namespace,
and to get some things from the namespace.  OSF does not license any of
the DCE core separately.  There is nothing to stop a vendor from doing
that, but given the inter-dependencies there seems to be little reason for
anyone to do so.

>Also, what is the difference between DEC's DCE and OSF's DCE?

They probably have slightly different sets of bugs. :-)  More seriously,
DEC's DCE is a productized version of the source that OSF sells.  At the
recent member meeting, a whole slew of vendors was showing full DCE inter-
operability.  I don't know all the names, so I won't type them here but it
was running on everything from a Windows NT machine through most major
workstations up to an IBM MVS machine (sitting in a trailer outside the
hall).
