I'm sorry, I should have explained better.  IMHO, this area is one of the
least-well documented and explained and matters are not helped by the (infamous)
timop example program.

I have no problem with creating an Object UUID to identify a particular object
instance (which is what the original question is about).

I have no problem that there should be the concept of server of a particular type
(although it is arguable whether the `type' isn't really just the interface
presented).

It is sensible that a server should be able to present multiple interfaces.

What confuses the heck out of me is why DCE provides the facility to generate and
make known (via the name service or otherwise) an Object UUID but then have the
facility to generate a Type UUID, which is registered with the RPC runtime, and
then an association created between Type and Object UUIDs so that there is
effectively a level of indirection created to no good effect.  It is not clear
to me that there is any occasion where the associate between Type and Object
UUID buys you any more flexibility that you might otherwise have.

I guess what I *really* want is a good, clear, concrete example of where it
is *necessary* to use both Type and Object UUIDs.  I can't really see where Type
UUIDs are necessary in the example you quote above.  If a server provides the
"print_text" interface you can change whatever you like behind that interface
provided you keep the semantics the same.  Clients don't have to change.

