In <1vn9bo$ce7@zaphod.axion.bt.co.uk> nwinton@axion.bt.co.uk (Neil Winton) writes:
>(although it is arguable whether the `type' isn't really just the interface
>presented).

No, a type is not the interface but the implementation of that interface.

Let me try again with my printer example.  Suppose I have each printer as
a UUID.  Suppose I have a print_text operation.  That does this:
	print_text(uuid_t printer, char *text)
	{
	    if (is_a_postscript_printer(printer))
		convert_to_postscript_and_print(text);
	    else if (is_a_daisy_wheel_printer(printer))
		send_text_to_printer(text);
	}

This can get kind of messy when you have more than two types -- every
function that is printer-specific must have a cascading set of if/elseif
statements to handle all the cases -- and hard to administer when you have
many objects.

Instead, your server says at the beginning "uuida is a postscript printer"
and "uuidb is a daisywheel printer" and so on.  Then, when a print_text
operation comes in, the RPC runtime automatically invokes print_text_ps
and print_text_daisy.  (You have to write those routines, and associate
them with the postscript and daisywheel manager dispatch table (MEPV).)

It is analogous to the standard problem of writing generic code in C:
you have to put switches all over the place, or use C++ where the
compiler does it for you automatically.
	/r$


